WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.072
Imagine checkout is healthy,

00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:04.841
but the shipping carrier suddenly takes eight

00:00:04.841 --> 00:00:07.548
seconds to respond. Customers keep arriving.

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:10.009
Threads pile up. Soon the whole checkout

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:10.809
feels broken.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:13.253
At eighty shipping requests per second,

00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:14.980
with eight seconds of waiting,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.340
roughly six hundred forty requests can be

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.930
outstanding. That is a capacity illustration,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.369
not a measured benchmark.

00:00:21.569 --> 00:00:23.374
First, bound network waiting.

00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:25.989
Set explicit connection and read timeouts,

00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:28.541
and enforce an overall checkout deadline.

00:00:28.541 --> 00:00:30.595
A timeout does not limit how many

00:00:30.595 --> 00:00:31.840
calls start at once.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.425
Next, isolate the shipping dependency.

00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:37.061
A semaphore bulkhead admits at most twenty

00:00:37.061 --> 00:00:39.697
concurrent calls per application instance.

00:00:39.697 --> 00:00:41.329
Excess requests fail fast,

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:43.338
protecting the rest of checkout.

00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:45.647
A circuit breaker measures failures.

00:00:45.647 --> 00:00:48.049
When its configured threshold is reached,

00:00:48.049 --> 00:00:50.510
it opens and stops repeated carrier calls.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.971
Remember: a breaker alone does not enforce

00:00:52.971 --> 00:00:54.787
a timeout or concurrency limit.

00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:57.103
After a wait, the breaker allows a

00:00:57.103 --> 00:00:59.468
few probe requests in half open state.

00:00:59.468 --> 00:01:00.961
If the carrier recovers,

00:01:00.961 --> 00:01:03.388
normal traffic resumes. If probes fail,

00:01:03.388 --> 00:01:04.881
the breaker opens again.

00:01:05.081 --> 00:01:07.111
For checkout, the fallback is not a

00:01:07.111 --> 00:01:08.329
made up shipping fee.

00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:11.402
Keep the cart, show shipping temporarily unavailable,

00:01:11.402 --> 00:01:13.490
and do not collect payment until the

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.882
final total is verified.

00:01:15.082 --> 00:01:18.204
Timeouts bound waiting. Bulkheads bound concurrency.

00:01:18.204 --> 00:01:20.607
Circuit breakers bound repeated failure.

00:01:20.607 --> 00:01:23.369
Together they stop one unhealthy provider from

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:25.771
taking down a healthy commerce platform.

00:01:25.771 --> 00:01:27.813
Test the behavior under real load.

